%!testlib
% What == does with a structure that contains itself, run through
% `xpost -q --no-sandbox -d null` by run-ps-test.sh.
%
% The printer descends into each array it meets. A structure that
% contains itself has no bottom, so two things are bounded: how deep the
% descent goes, and how much printing one == does. Past either an array
% prints as an array rather than being entered, and an element list stops
% short. Finite structures are unaffected -- the depth is deeper than
% ordinary nesting reaches, and the budget is far past what a program
% prints on purpose.


% a string to print into, so the output can be examined rather than
% merely produced
/out 4096 string def
/captured 0 def

% --------------------------------------------------- a structure containing itself

/a [ 1 2 3 ] def
a 0 a put
{ a == } (a self-referential array prints and returns) works

/d 3 dict def
d /self d put
{ d == } (a self-referential dictionary prints and returns) works

% Two references to itself, not one. A depth cap alone leaves the printer
% re-expanding both branches at every level, so the array is walked
% 2^depth times -- a tiny finite structure turned into unbounded work,
% reachable automatically when handleerror dumps such an array off the
% operand stack. The printing budget bounds it, so this returns rather
% than runs.
/b [ 0 0 ] def
b 0 b put
b 1 b put
{ b == } (an array with two references to itself prints and returns) works

% Wide as well as self-referential. Bounding how deep the descent goes
% does not bound what the descent does: one array entered is as much
% work as it is long, and a wide one referring to itself twice has every
% element of it re-expanded down both branches at every level. What is
% printed is counted, not just how deep the printer went or how many
% arrays it entered, so this returns.
/w 2000 array def
0 1 1999 { w exch 0 put } for
w 0 w put
w 1 w put
{ w == } (a wide array with two references to itself prints and returns) works

% A string costs its length to print -- a byte at a time, with its
% escapes -- so a structure reaching the same long string over and over
% is work in proportion to what it prints rather than to how many
% objects it printed. Counted the same way, so this returns too.
/big 60000 string def
0 1 59999 { big exch 65 put } for
/t [ 0 0 0 ] def
t 0 big put
t 1 t put
t 2 t put
{ t == } (a self-referential array holding a long string prints and returns) works

% two arrays that contain each other
/p [ 1 2 ] def
/q [ 3 4 ] def
p 0 q put
q 0 p put
{ p == } (two arrays containing each other print) works

% --------------------------------------------------------- finite structures

{ [ 1 [ 2 [ 3 [ 4 [ 5 ] ] ] ] ] == } (a finitely nested array prints) works
{ [ 1 2 3 ] == } (a flat array prints) works
{ 1 2 3 3 packedarray == } (a packed array prints) works
{ 1 2 /add cvx 3 packedarray cvx == } (an executable packed array prints) works
{ { 1 2 add } == } stopped not (a procedure prints) assert
{ [ (a) /b 1 2.5 true null ] == } (an array of every simple type prints) works

% nesting well beyond anything ordinary, still finite, still entered
% rather than abbreviated: twelve deep
/deep [ 1 ] def
12 { [ deep ] /deep exch def } repeat
{ deep == } (an array twelve deep prints) works

verdict
